title: Text search in the store
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Trigrams give a text search its candidates — each trigram by value while few texts hold it, by metarecord once many do, a write filing its ids at once — and a page without a count checks the text only where the sort looks.

A `matches` (regex) or `osm` leaf on a field with many distinct values — file names —
cannot scan them all at each keystroke. Each field's texts (strings, `tree_ref`
names) are indexed by the trigrams of their lower-cased form. A search's ''required
literals'' — the literal runs every match contains, extracted from the pattern's
syntax tree (a case-insensitive letter counting as the letter), or an `osm` term —
give trigrams, and the trigrams give candidates, which are then matched against the
real predicate. A pattern with no literal of three characters falls back to a scan of
the distinct values, restricted to the candidates of the enclosing `and`. A text over
512 bytes is not split: its id joins the field's long-text set, which every search
checks.

!! Candidates only

A trigram entry only ever names a ''candidate'', and every candidate is checked. One
too many costs a check; one missing would be a wrong answer. So a text that changes
or goes — a tag removed, a file renamed or deleted — leaves its trigrams where they
are: only a text ''coming'' writes. `mf repo reindex` drops what is stale.

This is what makes the index cheap to write. A file's texts hold some twenty
trigrams; kept exact per metarecord, each write touched twenty scattered keys, and
each removal the same twenty again.

!! Two modes per trigram

Each trigram of a field is kept one of two ways ([[Store tables]]): by value, its
list in `index_trigram_texts`, or by metarecord, its bitmap in
`index_trigram_holders`. The field's state in `index_trigram_state` says whether
any of its trigrams is by metarecord and which are turning; the ids of a text too
long to split are in `index_long_texts`.

''By value'' — how every trigram starts. Its key (field · trigram · 0) holds the
''list of the distinct texts'' holding it (their value keys — a position's name
being its value), in one value: a trigram is read in one key. A text new to the
field adds itself to the list of each of its trigrams; a text the field holds
already writes nothing — tagging a file with an existing tag writes nothing. A list
holds at most 64 texts (`GRAM_TEXTS_MAX`, a starting point to be measured), so
rewriting it is cheap and a search reads it whole: it intersects the lists of its
trigrams by value, tests each text left once — on its key, no metarecord is read —
and unions the postings of those that match. That answer is exact, with nothing
pending.

''By metarecord'' — a trigram in most texts of its field, `fil`, `ile` or `txt`
among file names: by value its list would be the field. Its holders are a bitmap of
''ids'', in chunks, which the write gaining a text brings up to date ''at once'' —
the text new to the field or not, its id joins the bitmap of each of its trigrams by
metarecord, a key per trigram; a write to many records
([[Mass writes in the store]]) files its ids chunk by chunk, a key per trigram and chunk. A search
intersects the bitmaps of its trigrams by metarecord — only the chunks of the
candidates the enclosing `and` hands it —: candidates, each checked on its own rows.

Until November 2026 a write only added its id to the field's ''pending'' set, one key,
and the fold filed it. That saved the write a key per trigram but not the work —
the fold did it, and read the record again to find its trigrams — and every search
checked the pending ids in the meantime: after a first reconcile, or fifty new files,
until the repository was quiet long enough for an idle fold
([[Folding in idle time]]), a search scanned the field's values (2 237 keys instead of 1 275 for
`file1` among 2 000 names after 50 new ones, `tests/kv_cost.rs`).

A search with trigrams in both modes takes the texts side: a text matching the
pattern holds every trigram, so it is in every list, and those lists are short. Its
trigrams by metarecord only answer a search none of whose trigrams is by value.

''Turning.'' A list growing past 64 texts is frozen (field · trigram · 2) and its
trigram added to the field's turning ones; from then on the writes file their ids in
its bitmap, as for a trigram by metarecord. A trigram turning narrows nothing. The
next fold, the index writes folded first so that the postings are exact — a write to
many records leaves them pending, which is why the turn waits for a fold — gives its
bitmap the holders of the frozen list's texts and drops the list. A trigram never turns back. The mode
being a trigram's, a field of file names keeps its rare trigrams — the digits of
`file12.txt` — by value: before November 2026 the mode was the field's, chosen on
the mean number of texts per trigram, and file names short enough stayed by value
whole, every search among them joining lists of a thousand texts.

!! The fold

`KvStore::fold` turns the trigrams turning, and a reindex ends with one, so a
reindexed repository — one converted from the former format included — has none.
Otherwise the daemon folds once the repository is quiet, one field's turning
trigrams per step ([[Folding in idle time]]), and `mf repo fold` at once: until then,
a search whose trigrams are all turning is answered by a scan of the field's values.

`mf repo check` cannot hold the trigrams to a rebuild entry by entry, since stale
ones are allowed: it verifies that no text lacks one — by value, the text in each of
its trigrams' lists; turning, the text in the frozen list or its id in the bitmap;
by metarecord, the id in each trigram's bitmap.

''A page without a count checks text lazily.'' A text leaf, or the text conjuncts of
an `and`, are split off the filter: what is evaluated is the candidate bitmap, and the
predicate is checked only on the ids the sort visits, stopping at the page's end. A
search as you type then costs its page — 0.5 to 3 ms on 5 million files, measured —
instead of its whole match set (170 to 530 ms); only a request asking for the total
pays for the whole set.

`tests/kv_text.rs` counts what a write puts in the trigram tables in each mode and holds the
answers to the oracle; `tests/kv_cost.rs`: on a folded repository a literal search
reads the same number of keys as on one eight times larger (it read the whole field
before: 2 004 keys against 16 004), and names written after the fold cost a key per
trigram at most and no search a check.
